{
  "rules": [
    "Uptrend overshoot: when the last 10–15 days show a steady rise, the model tends to project too steep/too high a continuation (especially beyond ~day 10). Apply a downward tilt that increases with horizon (small in days 1–5, larger in days 15–30).",
    "Spike/chase bias: after local highs or sharp rallies in the history window, the model ‘chases’ the move and keeps prices elevated too long. Add faster mean reversion: pull forecasts back toward the recent 10–20d average, particularly for horizons 5–20.",
    "Mid-horizon underreaction to rebounds: around trough-to-rebound phases (after a dip), the model sometimes undershoots the rebound in the 2–4 week window (seen where true prices re-accelerate while forecasts flatten). Use a mild upward correction when history shows a V-shape (recent low followed by consecutive higher closes).",
    "Timing/lag error: the model often gets direction right but is early/late on turning points by a few days. Smooth by shifting part of the forecast toward the last observed level (blend: 60–80% model, 20–40% last price) for days 1–7, and toward a moving average for days 8–30.",
    "Horizon-dependent bias: near-term (days 1–5) errors are small; errors widen with horizon, with a consistent positive bias in days ~10–30 during rising regimes. Implement regime filter: if 20d trend > 0, subtract an increasing bias; if trend < 0, reduce any negative bias (less aggressive downside)."
  ]
}
